// examples/quant/american_binomial — deterministic American option pricing:
// early exercise on a CRR lattice, the Barone-Adesi/Whaley quadratic
// approximation, its critical exercise boundary, and the early-exercise premium.
//
// Copyright 2025 STARGA Inc.
// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE crr_binomial.  `examples/quant/crr_binomial`
// already builds a recombining lattice and already takes a max() against
// intrinsic value when its `american` flag is set.  That file's subject is the
// LATTICE: an iterative, order-sensitive accumulation held bit-identical across
// substrates.  Repeating it here would add nothing.
//
// The subject of THIS file is the early-exercise decision itself, and three
// things that only become visible once you take it seriously:
//
//   1. THE OPTIMAL-STOPPING PROBLEM HAS NO CLOSED FORM.  There is no American
//      analogue of Black-Scholes.  The industry prices this with a lattice
//      (slow, convergent) or a closed-form APPROXIMATION (fast, biased).  This
//      file implements both and CROSS-CHECKS them, which is strictly stronger
//      than either alone: a lattice bug and an approximation bug would have to
//      agree to survive.
//
//   2. THE CRITICAL BOUNDARY IS THE ROOT OF A TRANSCENDENTAL EQUATION.  How
//      that root-find TERMINATES is a reproducibility question, not a
//      numerical-analysis nicety — see the comment above `baw_s_star_put`.
//
//   3. THE PREMIUM IS A DIFFERENCE OF TWO PRICES.  `american - european` is a
//      small number formed by subtracting two comparable larger ones — the
//      cancellation that makes a non-reproducible library report a different
//      hedge on two machines while both "prices" look fine to four decimals.
//
// WHAT IS AN APPROXIMATION HERE, STATED UP FRONT.  `baw_call` and `baw_put`
// implement Barone-Adesi & Whaley (1987).  They are NOT exact American prices
// and are not claimed to be.  BAW is a quadratic approximation to the
// early-exercise premium; its error is small near the money and for short
// maturities, and grows for long-dated, deep-in-the-money, high-carry options.
// The KATs below MEASURE that error against a converged lattice rather than
// asserting it away — see the CROSS-CHECK block, where the worst observed
// disagreement (a deep-ITM, r=0.10 put) is 0.062 in price, ~0.53% relative,
// and is asserted AS a band with that number written down. If a future change
// makes that gap grow, the check fails; the correct response is to investigate,
// never to widen the band.
//
// DETERMINISM PROPERTIES, ALL OF THEM NEGATIVE OR FIXED.
//   * No clock, no OS entropy, no global mutable state.  Every function is pure
//     in its arguments.
//   * Every loop has a trip count that is either a literal constant or an
//     explicit integer PARAMETER (the lattice step count `n`).  No loop's trip
//     count depends on an f64 value.  A price is therefore a fixed sequence of
//     operations for a fixed (n, flags), and that sequence is what the
//     cross-substrate fixture attests.
//   * Every reduction is a fixed left-fold in source order.  No reassociation.
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels below are duplicated VERBATIM
// from `examples/quant/black_scholes/main.mind` rather than imported.  mindc
// 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across a module boundary (roadmap 17.1): an
// imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as `(i64) -> i64` at the call site and
// miscompiles silently.  The duplication is byte-identical on purpose — if the
// two copies were allowed to drift, two quant examples would compute two
// different `exp` and the cross-example comparison in the KAT below would be
// meaningless.  Mirrors `examples/detmath_kat`.
// deferred: replace the KERNEL block with `import std.detmath;` once roadmap
// 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete everything between the KERNEL markers.
//
// THE `dm_` PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT COSMETIC.  A MIND function whose name
// matches a C99 libm symbol (`exp`, `log`, `pow`, `sin`, `cos`, `erf`, `erfc`)
// can be recognised by LLVM as that builtin and CONSTANT-FOLDED to the HOST
// libm when its argument is a compile-time constant — silently skipping the
// MIND body and producing a value this compiler never computed.  That defect is
// documented in this repo.  Every kernel here is named `dm_*` or `det_*`,
// outside the libm namespace.  Do not rename one for readability.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 branch condition (takes the then-branch
// unconditionally), so every guarded function would return through its leading
// NaN guard and every interpreted assertion would pass vacuously — a false
// green.  The native backend is correct, so the gate is build-and-run: `mindc
// run`, and THE EXIT CODE IS THE COUNT OF FAILED CHECKS.
//
// REFERENCES (original papers only):
//   Barone-Adesi, G. & Whaley, R. E. (1987). "Efficient Analytic Approximation
//     of American Option Values." Journal of Finance 42(2), 301-320.
//   Cox, J. C., Ross, S. A. & Rubinstein, M. (1979). "Option Pricing: A
//     Simplified Approach." Journal of Financial Economics 7(3), 229-263.
//   Merton, R. C. (1973). "Theory of Rational Option Pricing." Bell Journal of
//     Economics and Management Science 4(1), 141-183.  (Proves an American call
//     on a non-dividend-paying stock is never exercised early — the bit-exact
//     structural invariant asserted below, and the single sharpest check here.)
//   MacMillan, L. W. (1986). "Analytic Approximation for the American Put
//     Option." Advances in Futures and Options Research 1, 119-139.  (The put
//     case BAW generalises.)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated VERBATIM from examples/quant/black_scholes/main.mind (roadmap 17.1
// blocks importing f64 across modules).  Do not "improve" these: every
// polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner recurrence in source order.
// Reassociating one changes the last bit of every price downstream and breaks
// the cross-substrate hash.
fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

fn dm_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return sqrt(x);
}

// erf by its Maclaurin series, ascending fixed left-fold.  Certified |x| < 2.
fn erf_series(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let two_over_sqrtpi: f64 = 1.1283791670955126;
    let x2: f64 = x * x;
    let mut t: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut s: f64 = x * 1.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 1;
    while n < 60 {
        t = t * (0.0 - x2) / (n as f64);
        s = s + t / ((2 * n + 1) as f64);
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return two_over_sqrtpi * s;
}

// erfc by a fixed-depth backward continued fraction.  Certified x >= 1.
// The depth is static, never convergence-tested: a convergence test would be
// deterministic but would make the rounding sequence input-dependent.
fn erfc_cf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let sqrtpi: f64 = 1.7724538509055159;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 200;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = ((n as f64) * 0.5) / (x + acc);
        n = n - 1;
    }
    return det_exp(0.0 - x * x) / sqrtpi / (x + acc);
}

fn dm_erfc(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - inf() {
        return 2.0;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 2.0 - dm_erfc(0.0 - x);
    }
    if x < 1.0 {
        return 1.0 - erf_series(x);
    }
    return erfc_cf(x);
}

// Standard normal CDF.  Computed as 0.5*erfc(-x/sqrt(2)), NEVER as the
// reflected 1 - norm_cdf(-x): the reflected form cancels to exactly 0.0 in the
// left tail, which is precisely where a deep out-of-the-money option lives.
// See the deep-OTM KAT below, where the true call premium is 4.955e-12 and a
// reflected implementation would report a worthless option as exactly
// worthless, with no indication of error.
fn dm_norm_cdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt2: f64 = 0.7071067811865476;
    return 0.5 * dm_erfc(0.0 - x * inv_sqrt2);
}

// Standard normal PDF, phi(x) = exp(-x^2/2)/sqrt(2*pi).  Needed by gamma,
// vega and theta.  1/sqrt(2*pi) is a rounded constant, not a division.
fn dm_norm_pdf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    let inv_sqrt_2pi: f64 = 0.3989422804014327;
    return inv_sqrt_2pi * det_exp(0.0 - 0.5 * x * x);
}

// ============================ KERNEL END ===================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Small shared helpers.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn maxf(a: f64, b: f64) -> f64 {
    if a > b {
        return a;
    }
    return b;
}

fn minf(a: f64, b: f64) -> f64 {
    if a < b {
        return a;
    }
    return b;
}

// x^y for x > 0, as exp(y * log(x)).  Deliberately NOT named `pow`: see the
// libm-capture note in the header.  Only ever called here with x > 0 and a
// finite y, which is why there is no branch on a negative base — a negative
// base would need an integer-exponent path and this file never has one.
fn dm_powpos(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if y != y {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return dm_exp(y * dm_log(x));
}

// Shared precondition check for every pricing entry point below.  Violations
// return the canonical qNaN rather than a plausible-looking number: a silent
// numeric fallback on a degenerate input is how a pricing bug reaches
// production wearing a valid-looking price.
fn am_valid(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> i64 {
    if s != s {
        return 0;
    }
    if k != k {
        return 0;
    }
    if r != r {
        return 0;
    }
    if q != q {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return 0;
    }
    if t != t {
        return 0;
    }
    if s <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// European Black-Scholes-Merton leg.
//
// Present here NOT as a contribution — `examples/quant/black_scholes` owns that
// — but because two things in this file are DEFINED in terms of it:
//   * the early-exercise premium, which is american - european;
//   * the BAW approximation, whose whole structure is "European price plus a
//     quadratic approximation to the premium".
// Written in the same fixed evaluation order as black_scholes/main.mind: the d1
// numerator is summed left to right before the single division.  Regrouping is
// algebraically identical and numerically different.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eu_d1(s: f64, k: f64, b: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    // `b` is the COST OF CARRY, b = r - q.  BAW is stated in carry form in the
    // original paper, and using the same parameterisation in the European leg
    // keeps the two implementations textually comparable.
    let sq: f64 = sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    return (dm_log(s / k) + (b + 0.5 * sigma * sigma) * t) / sq;
}

pub fn eu_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if am_valid(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = eu_d1(s, k, r - q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(d1) - k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(d2);
}

pub fn eu_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if am_valid(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let d1: f64 = eu_d1(s, k, r - q, sigma, t);
    let d2: f64 = d1 - sigma * dm_sqrt(t);
    let df_r: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let df_q: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
    return k * df_r * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d2) - s * df_q * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d1);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LATTICE STORAGE.  The level buffer holds f64 values as their exact IEEE bit
// patterns in an i64 heap block, via the `__mind_f64_to_bits` /
// `__mind_bits_to_f64` reinterpret pair.  That is a lossless bit copy, NOT a
// numeric conversion: nothing is rounded on the way in or out.  mindc 0.10.2
// has no f64 load/store intrinsic, so this is the executable-subset route to a
// mutable f64 array.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn lvl_get(buf: i64, i: i64) -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(__mind_load_i64(buf + i * 8));
}

fn lvl_set(buf: i64, i: i64, v: f64) -> i64 {
    __mind_store_i64(buf + i * 8, __mind_f64_to_bits(v));
    return 0;
}

fn intrinsic(spot: f64, k: f64, is_call: i64) -> f64 {
    if is_call != 0 {
        return maxf(spot - k, 0.0);
    }
    return maxf(k - spot, 0.0);
}

// Underlying at lattice node (i, j): S * u^(2j - i).
//
// RECONSTRUCTED, NEVER ACCUMULATED.  Walking a running product across a level
// would be cheaper but its rounding error would depend on the traversal
// direction, making the lattice a function of HOW it is walked rather than of
// (i, j) alone.  Recomputing from S costs operations and buys order
// independence.  The loop's trip count is |2j - i|, an integer derived from
// integer node indices — never from an f64 — so the operation count is fixed
// for a fixed n.
fn node_spot(s: f64, u: f64, d: f64, i: i64, j: i64) -> f64 {
    // f64-param-init: seed the loop-carried binding via the exact identity
    // `s * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut sp: f64 = s * 1.0;
    let e: i64 = 2 * j - i;
    let mut c: i64 = 0;
    if e >= 0 {
        while c < e {
            sp = sp * u;
            c = c + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while c < (0 - e) {
            sp = sp * d;
            c = c + 1;
        }
    }
    return sp;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The binomial option pricing model (BOPM) on a Cox-Ross-Rubinstein lattice,
// with the early-exercise test applied at EVERY interior node.
//
//   dt = T/n,  u = e^(sigma*sqrt(dt)),  d = 1/u,
//   p  = (e^((r-q)*dt) - d) / (u - d),   disc = e^(-r*dt)
//
// THE ACCUMULATION ORDER IS PINNED, AND THIS IS THE CONTRACT.  Each interior
// node is evaluated as exactly
//
//     continuation = disc * (p * up + pm * dn)
//     value        = max(continuation, intrinsic(node_spot))
//
// in that order: the two probability-weighted terms are summed FIRST, then a
// single discount multiply, then one comparison against intrinsic value.  The
// algebraically identical regrouping `disc * p * up + disc * pm * dn` is a
// different sequence of roundings and produces different bits.  Levels are
// swept from index 0 upward within a level, and levels from i = n-1 down to 0.
// Do not reorder either sweep and do not regroup the expression.
//
// WHY EARLY EXERCISE DIFFERS FROM A PLAIN EXPECTATION.  The max() is a
// DISCONTINUOUS function of the continuation value.  A one-ulp difference near
// the exercise boundary does not merely perturb the output by one ulp — it can
// flip which branch is taken, replacing the discounted expectation with
// intrinsic value wholesale.  A float-nondeterministic implementation can
// therefore disagree with ITSELF about WHETHER TO EXERCISE, not just about how
// much the position is worth.  That is qualitatively worse than a rounding
// difference, and it is why this example exists.
//
// Preconditions: finite S,K > 0, sigma > 0, T > 0, 1 <= n <= 1024, and a
// risk-neutral probability p inside [0,1].  Violations return the canonical
// qNaN.  The p bound is not pedantry: outside [0,1] the lattice admits
// arbitrage and the "price" it produces is meaningless, so it must be refused
// rather than reported.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn bopm(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64, is_call: i64, american: i64) -> f64 {
    if am_valid(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if n < 1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if n > 1024 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }

    let dt: f64 = t / (n as f64);
    let u: f64 = dm_exp(sigma * dm_sqrt(dt));
    let d: f64 = 1.0 / u;
    let disc: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - r * dt);
    let growth: f64 = dm_exp((r - q) * dt);
    let p: f64 = (growth - d) / (u - d);
    let pm: f64 = 1.0 - p;

    if p < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if p > 1.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }

    let buf: i64 = __mind_alloc((n + 1) * 8);

    // --- terminal level i = n ---------------------------------------------
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    while j <= n {
        lvl_set(buf, j, intrinsic(node_spot(s, u, d, n, j), k, is_call));
        j = j + 1;
    }

    // --- backward induction, level i = n-1 down to 0 -----------------------
    let mut i: i64 = n - 1;
    while i >= 0 {
        let mut m: i64 = 0;
        while m <= i {
            let up: f64 = lvl_get(buf, m + 1);
            let dn: f64 = lvl_get(buf, m);
            let mut v: f64 = disc * (p * up + pm * dn);
            if american != 0 {
                v = maxf(v, intrinsic(node_spot(s, u, d, i, m), k, is_call));
            }
            lvl_set(buf, m, v);
            m = m + 1;
        }
        i = i - 1;
    }

    return lvl_get(buf, 0);
}

// Public American entry points.  Separate call/put functions rather than a
// flag, because the two have different structural theorems attached (Merton's
// no-early-exercise result applies to the call only) and a reader should not
// have to trace a boolean to know which one they are looking at.
pub fn bopm_american_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    return bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 1, 1);
}

pub fn bopm_american_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    return bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 0, 1);
}

pub fn bopm_european_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    return bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 1, 0);
}

pub fn bopm_european_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    return bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 0, 0);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CRITICAL EXERCISE BOUNDARY S*.
//
// S* is the underlying price at which immediate exercise becomes exactly as
// good as holding.  For a put it is the LOW price below which you exercise; for
// a call (with q > 0) the HIGH price above which you exercise.  It solves a
// transcendental equation with no closed form:
//
//   put :  K - S* = p_euro(S*) - (1 - e^(-qT) N(-d1(S*))) * S* / q1
//   call:  S* - K = c_euro(S*) + (1 - e^(-qT) N( d1(S*))) * S* / q2
//
// WHY THE ITERATION COUNT IS A FIXED LITERAL AND NOT A TOLERANCE TEST.
//
// The textbook solve is `while |f(S)| > eps { newton step }`.  That is
// deterministic for any single input — same input, same answer, every time —
// and is still the wrong choice here, for three reasons:
//
//   1. THE TRIP COUNT BECOMES A FUNCTION OF THE DATA.  Two nearby inputs
//      converge in different numbers of steps, so two prices are computed by
//      two DIFFERENT sequences of floating-point operations.  The function
//      stops having a fixed operation count, and there is no longer a single
//      sequence for the cross-substrate fixture to attest.
//
//   2. THE STOPPING TEST IS ITSELF A FLOAT COMPARISON NEAR A BOUNDARY — the
//      same discontinuous-branch hazard described above the lattice.  If the
//      last residual lands within one ulp of eps, two substrates agreeing to
//      one ulp can still take different iteration counts and diverge by far
//      more.  The tolerance test converts a harmless rounding difference into
//      a structural one.
//
//   3. IT MAKES COST UNPREDICTABLE.  A fixed trip count is the same work for
//      every input: timing-uniform, and auditable by reading the source.
//
// The cost is wasted iterations on easy inputs.  We accept it.  64 steps is
// chosen with margin: Newton here is locally quadratic and BAW's own seed is
// close, so f64 precision is reached in well under 16 steps for every KAT
// case.  The remaining iterations are idempotent to the last bit once the
// iterate is a fixed point of the map — which is why over-iterating is safe
// here and would NOT be safe for a method that oscillates.
//
// THE GUARD RAILS ARE PART OF THE DETERMINISM STORY.  Each step clamps the
// iterate into a fixed positive interval before the next evaluation.  Without
// it a wild Newton step could produce a non-positive S, whose `dm_log` is -inf,
// after which every later iterate is NaN and the fixed count cheerfully runs 63
// more times on garbage.  Clamping is not a convergence heuristic — it does not
// move the fixed point — it only keeps the iteration in the map's domain.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn baw_newton_steps() -> i64 {
    return 64;
}

// q1 / q2 are the roots of the quadratic that gives BAW its name.  They come
// from substituting a power-law ansatz S^q into the Black-Scholes PDE with the
// time-derivative term approximated away — that approximation is precisely what
// makes BAW an approximation.
//   M = 2r/sigma^2,  Nc = 2b/sigma^2,  KK = 1 - e^(-rT)
//   q1 = (-(Nc-1) - sqrt((Nc-1)^2 + 4M/KK)) / 2    (negative root, put)
//   q2 = (-(Nc-1) + sqrt((Nc-1)^2 + 4M/KK)) / 2    (positive root, call)
fn baw_q1(r: f64, b: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let mm: f64 = 2.0 * r / sig2;
    let nc: f64 = 2.0 * b / sig2;
    let kk: f64 = 1.0 - dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let disc: f64 = (nc - 1.0) * (nc - 1.0) + 4.0 * mm / kk;
    return (0.0 - (nc - 1.0) - dm_sqrt(disc)) / 2.0;
}

fn baw_q2(r: f64, b: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let mm: f64 = 2.0 * r / sig2;
    let nc: f64 = 2.0 * b / sig2;
    let kk: f64 = 1.0 - dm_exp(0.0 - r * t);
    let disc: f64 = (nc - 1.0) * (nc - 1.0) + 4.0 * mm / kk;
    return (0.0 - (nc - 1.0) + dm_sqrt(disc)) / 2.0;
}

// The perpetual-option roots, used only to build the Newton SEED.  Same
// quadratic with KK = 1 (T -> infinity).
fn baw_q1_inf(r: f64, b: f64, sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let mm: f64 = 2.0 * r / sig2;
    let nc: f64 = 2.0 * b / sig2;
    let disc: f64 = (nc - 1.0) * (nc - 1.0) + 4.0 * mm;
    return (0.0 - (nc - 1.0) - dm_sqrt(disc)) / 2.0;
}

fn baw_q2_inf(r: f64, b: f64, sigma: f64) -> f64 {
    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let mm: f64 = 2.0 * r / sig2;
    let nc: f64 = 2.0 * b / sig2;
    let disc: f64 = (nc - 1.0) * (nc - 1.0) + 4.0 * mm;
    return (0.0 - (nc - 1.0) + dm_sqrt(disc)) / 2.0;
}

// Critical price for the AMERICAN PUT.  Fixed 64-step Newton on
//   f(S) = K - S - p_euro(S) + (1 - e^(-qT) N(-d1)) * S / q1 = 0
// using BAW's own analytic Jacobian `bi` (never a finite difference: a bumped
// derivative would inherit a step-size choice and two prices' worth of
// rounding).
pub fn baw_s_star_put(k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if k != k {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t != t {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if r <= 0.0 {
        // With a non-positive rate there is no carry benefit to exercising a
        // put early and the boundary degenerates.  Refuse rather than iterate
        // on a divide-by-zero in M = 2r/sigma^2.
        return canonical_qnan();
    }

    let b: f64 = r - q;
    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let q1: f64 = baw_q1(r, b, sigma, t);
    let q1i: f64 = baw_q1_inf(r, b, sigma);

    // BAW's seed: su is the perpetual-put boundary, h shrinks it toward K as
    // maturity shortens.  A good seed is why 64 steps is generous rather than
    // marginal.
    let su: f64 = k / (1.0 - 2.0 / (0.0 - q1i));
    let h: f64 = (b * t - 2.0 * sigma * sqt) * k / (k - su);
    let mut si: f64 = su + (k - su) * dm_exp(h);

    // Domain clamp for the seed itself.  The put boundary always lies in
    // (0, K]; a seed outside that interval is a bug in the seed formula, not a
    // legitimate starting point, and must not be allowed to poison the fixed
    // iteration.
    si = maxf(si, 0.0000001 * k);
    si = minf(si, k);

    let mut it: i64 = 0;
    while it < baw_newton_steps() {
        let d1: f64 = (dm_log(si / k) + (b + 0.5 * sig2) * t) / (sigma * sqt);
        let nd1n: f64 = dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d1);
        let pdf1: f64 = dm_norm_pdf(0.0 - d1);
        let dfq: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
        let pv: f64 = eu_put(si, k, r, q, sigma, t);
        // rhs and bi written in BAW's own grouping.  Do not regroup.
        let rhs: f64 = pv - (1.0 - dfq * nd1n) * si / q1;
        let bi: f64 = (0.0 - dfq * nd1n * (1.0 - 1.0 / q1)) - (1.0 + dfq * pdf1 / (sigma * sqt)) / q1;
        let nxt: f64 = (k - rhs + bi * si) / (1.0 + bi);
        // Clamp back into the domain.  See the header note: this does not move
        // the fixed point, it only keeps `dm_log` in its domain so a wild step
        // cannot turn the remaining iterations into NaN arithmetic.
        si = maxf(nxt, 0.0000001 * k);
        si = minf(si, k);
        it = it + 1;
    }
    return si;
}

// Critical price for the AMERICAN CALL.  Only meaningful when b < r (i.e.
// q > 0): with q = 0 the carry never justifies early exercise, the boundary is
// at infinity, and this returns the canonical qNaN rather than a large
// plausible number.  That refusal is the computational shadow of Merton 1973.
pub fn baw_s_star_call(k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if k != k {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if sigma != sigma {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t != t {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if k <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if sigma <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if r <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let b: f64 = r - q;
    if b >= r {
        // q <= 0: no dividend, no early exercise, no finite boundary.
        return canonical_qnan();
    }

    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let q2: f64 = baw_q2(r, b, sigma, t);
    let q2i: f64 = baw_q2_inf(r, b, sigma);

    let su: f64 = k / (1.0 - 2.0 / q2i);
    let h: f64 = (0.0 - (b * t + 2.0 * sigma * sqt)) * k / (su - k);
    let mut si: f64 = k + (su - k) * (1.0 - dm_exp(h));

    // The call boundary always lies above K.  Clamp to [K, 1e6*K] for the same
    // domain reason as the put.
    si = maxf(si, k);
    si = minf(si, 1000000.0 * k);

    let mut it: i64 = 0;
    while it < baw_newton_steps() {
        let d1: f64 = (dm_log(si / k) + (b + 0.5 * sig2) * t) / (sigma * sqt);
        let nd1: f64 = dm_norm_cdf(d1);
        let pdf1: f64 = dm_norm_pdf(d1);
        let dfq: f64 = dm_exp(0.0 - q * t);
        let cv: f64 = eu_call(si, k, r, q, sigma, t);
        let rhs: f64 = cv + (1.0 - dfq * nd1) * si / q2;
        let bi: f64 = dfq * nd1 * (1.0 - 1.0 / q2) + (1.0 - dfq * pdf1 / (sigma * sqt)) / q2;
        let nxt: f64 = (k + rhs - bi * si) / (1.0 - bi);
        si = maxf(nxt, k);
        si = minf(si, 1000000.0 * k);
        it = it + 1;
    }
    return si;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BARONE-ADESI & WHALEY (1987) QUADRATIC APPROXIMATION.
//
// THIS IS AN APPROXIMATION.  Not a price.  The construction is: take the exact
// European value, then add a closed-form approximation to the early-exercise
// premium obtained by dropping a term from the PDE that governs the premium.
// The dropped term is small when the option is near the money and short-dated,
// and is NOT small for long-dated deep-in-the-money options with large carry.
//
//   call, S <  S*:  C_euro(S) + A2 * (S/S*)^q2,  A2 =  (S*/q2)(1 - e^(-qT)N( d1*))
//   call, S >= S*:  S - K                        (exercise immediately)
//   put,  S >  S*:  P_euro(S) + A1 * (S/S*)^q1,  A1 = -(S*/q1)(1 - e^(-qT)N(-d1*))
//   put,  S <= S*:  K - S
//
// ERROR PROFILE, MEASURED NOT ASSERTED.  Against a converged CRR lattice:
//     ATM put  S=K=100 r=.05 sig=.20 T=1    ~0.010  (0.17%)
//     ATM put  S=K=100 r=.08 sig=.20 T=.5   ~0.004  (0.10%)
//     ITM put  S=90 K=100 r=.10 sig=.25 T=1 ~0.062  (0.53%)  <- worst
//     ATM call S=K=100 r=.05 q=.07 T=1      ~0.045  (0.51%)
// Those numbers are the CROSS-CHECK bands below, so a reader can see the cost
// of choosing BAW over a lattice with the error in front of them rather than
// from folklore.
//
// WHY BOTH, OPERATIONALLY.  A lattice at n=256 is ~33000 node evaluations; BAW
// is a fixed 64-step Newton plus a handful of transcendentals — two orders of
// magnitude cheaper, and what a real-time risk system uses to revalue a book.
// The lattice is what that system is CALIBRATED against.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub fn baw_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if am_valid(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let b: f64 = r - q;
    if b >= r {
        // Merton 1973: with no dividend the American call is never exercised
        // early, so the exact European value IS the American value.  Returning
        // the European price here is not a shortcut or a fallback — it is the
        // correct answer, and it is why the q=0 KAT below is a BIT-EXACT
        // equality rather than a band.
        return eu_call(s, k, r, q, sigma, t);
    }
    if r <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }

    let sstar: f64 = baw_s_star_call(k, r, q, sigma, t);
    if sstar != sstar {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if s >= sstar {
        return s - k;
    }

    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let q2: f64 = baw_q2(r, b, sigma, t);
    let d1s: f64 = (dm_log(sstar / k) + (b + 0.5 * sig2) * t) / (sigma * sqt);
    let a2: f64 = (sstar / q2) * (1.0 - dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_cdf(d1s));
    return eu_call(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) + a2 * dm_powpos(s / sstar, q2);
}

pub fn baw_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64) -> f64 {
    if am_valid(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if r <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let b: f64 = r - q;

    let sstar: f64 = baw_s_star_put(k, r, q, sigma, t);
    if sstar != sstar {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if s <= sstar {
        return k - s;
    }

    let sig2: f64 = sigma * sigma;
    let sqt: f64 = dm_sqrt(t);
    let q1: f64 = baw_q1(r, b, sigma, t);
    let d1s: f64 = (dm_log(sstar / k) + (b + 0.5 * sig2) * t) / (sigma * sqt);
    let a1: f64 = (0.0 - (sstar / q1)) * (1.0 - dm_exp(0.0 - q * t) * dm_norm_cdf(0.0 - d1s));
    return eu_put(s, k, r, q, sigma, t) + a1 * dm_powpos(s / sstar, q1);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EARLY-EXERCISE PREMIUM: american - european, on the SAME lattice.
//
// The subtraction is deliberately between two LATTICE prices, never between a
// lattice American and a closed-form European.  Those carry different
// discretisation error, and differencing them would report the discretisation
// gap as if it were economic value: at n=64 the lattice European call is
// 10.4194 against the closed form's 10.4506, so a mixed difference would show a
// spurious -0.031 "premium" on an option whose true premium is exactly zero.
// One lattice for both legs cancels that error to first order and makes the
// premium a statement about early exercise rather than about step count.
//
// This is where reproducibility bites hardest: the premium is a difference of
// two comparable numbers, so it has far fewer significant digits than either
// leg.  Two implementations agreeing to 1e-13 on each price can disagree in the
// fourth digit of the premium.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub fn early_exercise_premium_put(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    let am: f64 = bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 0, 1);
    let eu: f64 = bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 0, 0);
    return am - eu;
}

pub fn early_exercise_premium_call(s: f64, k: f64, r: f64, q: f64, sigma: f64, t: f64, n: i64) -> f64 {
    let am: f64 = bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 1, 1);
    let eu: f64 = bopm(s, k, r, q, sigma, t, n, 1, 0);
    return am - eu;
}

// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// Four classes of check, in increasing order of how much they survive:
//
//   1. STRUCTURAL — theorems that must hold whatever the arithmetic does, and
//      which therefore SURVIVE A BAD ORACLE.  These are the most valuable
//      checks in the file and they come first for that reason.  A reference
//      value can be wrong; Merton's no-early-exercise result cannot.
//   2. VALUE      — against references, within a stated band.
//   3. CROSS-CHECK— BAW against a converged lattice.  Two independent methods
//      disagreeing beyond the stated band is a real finding.
//   4. ADVERSARIAL— a deliberately WRONG reference must be REJECTED.  A
//      spurious acceptance is added straight into `fails`.  Without this block
//      every band above could be passing because it is too wide.
//
// PROVENANCE, STATED EXACTLY, BECAUSE THE LEGS ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF NUMBER:
//
//   * EUROPEAN values are a closed form, from an independent 50-digit oracle
//     (mpmath, mp.dps=50) rounded once to f64.  Exact references.
//
//   * LATTICE values are NOT a closed form and have no exact reference.  An
//     American price has no analytic solution — that is the premise of this
//     file.  The oracle runs the SAME recombining lattice with the same
//     node-spot rule at 50 digits, so the discretisation is identical and only
//     the arithmetic differs.  A check against it proves the f64
//     implementation accumulates no error, and proves NOTHING about whether n
//     steps is enough.  That second question is what the CONVERGENCE block is
//     for, and the two must not be confused.
//
//   * BAW values are a closed-form approximation, oracle-checked like the
//     European leg — but the quantity they approximate is not the quantity the
//     lattice computes, which is why the CROSS-CHECK bands are orders of
//     magnitude wider.  That width is approximation error, not implementation
//     slack, and it is written down.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED.  These are
// tolerance bands, so they are structurally blind to any change that perturbs a
// result by less than the band — including a reassociation that is
// algebraically identical and numerically different.  That blindness was
// MEASURED on this file, not assumed.  Rewriting the pinned inner recurrence
//     disc * (p * up + pm * dn)   as   disc * p * up + disc * pm * dn
// changes the American put price by 7 ulp at n=256 (6.087258327709413 ->
// ...407) and 11 ulp at n=64 (6.077526066883204 -> ...194), and the whole file
// still exits 0.  A real bit-level divergence, invisible to every band here.
//
// Tightening them is NOT the fix: the lattice bands must stay wide enough
// (~65536 ulp at n=256) to absorb the implementation's own honest accumulated
// rounding over ~33000 dependent node evaluations, and tightened past that they
// would fail for correct code.  Detecting reassociation requires a BIT-EXACT
// reference, and the right home for that is the cross-substrate CI fixture
// (tests/cross_substrate_identity.rs), which compares hashes of emitted output
// across x86 `avx2` and ARM `neon` rather than comparing against a value.  A
// hash has no tolerance to hide in.
//
// Stated as a rule: this KAT proves the pricing is CORRECT; the fixture proves
// it is REPRODUCIBLE.  Neither substitutes for the other, and a reader must not
// read a green KAT as evidence of byte-identity.
//
// ONE MORE GAP, SPECIFIC TO AMERICAN PRICING.  These checks verify the PRICE,
// not the exercise POLICY.  Two lattices disagreeing about whether to exercise
// at a handful of nodes near the boundary can still agree on price to 1e-12,
// because at the boundary continuation and intrinsic value are equal BY
// DEFINITION — that is what makes it the boundary.  A desk trades the policy,
// not the price.  Testing it would need the exercise region emitted and
// compared, which is out of scope; the `baw_s_star_put` checks are the closest
// this file gets, and they cover the approximation's boundary, not the
// lattice's.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// `cond != 0` must hold.  Returns 1 on FAILURE so it adds into `fails`
// directly, matching the `1 - chk_*` idiom used for the value checks.
fn must(cond: i64) -> i64 {
    if cond != 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // =====================================================================
    // 1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITIES.  These survive a bad oracle.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- MERTON 1973, AND THIS IS THE SINGLE BEST CHECK IN THE FILE. ------
    // An American call on a NON-dividend-paying underlying (q = 0) is never
    // exercised early: the intrinsic value S - K is always below the
    // continuation value, because exercising throws away both the remaining
    // time value AND the interest earned by deferring payment of the strike.
    // So the early-exercise max() must NEVER bind, and the American price must
    // equal the European price TO THE LAST BIT.
    //
    // Asserted BIT-EXACT, not as a band, and that is what makes it sharp: it
    // catches a max() that binds even once in ~4700 nodes by even one ulp, a
    // condition no tolerance band could detect.  It simultaneously tests the
    // lattice, intrinsic(), node_spot() and the American/European dispatch,
    // and needs no reference value at all.
    let ac96: f64 = bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96);
    let ec96: f64 = bopm_european_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96);
    fails = fails + must(__mind_f64_to_bits(ac96) == __mind_f64_to_bits(ec96));

    // Repeated at a second, ITM point so the equality is not an accident of
    // one moneyness, and at an odd step count so it is not an accident of a
    // power-of-two lattice.
    let ac2: f64 = bopm_american_call(120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.25, 2.0, 77);
    let ec2: f64 = bopm_european_call(120.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.25, 2.0, 77);
    fails = fails + must(__mind_f64_to_bits(ac2) == __mind_f64_to_bits(ec2));

    // ... and the corresponding claim on the closed-form side: BAW at q = 0
    // must return exactly the European value, because its `b >= r` branch is
    // the computational statement of the same theorem.
    fails = fails + must(__mind_f64_to_bits(baw_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)) == __mind_f64_to_bits(eu_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));

    // ... and the boundary refuses to exist there, rather than returning a
    // large plausible number.  A caller that gets a finite S* for a q=0 call
    // would silently build an exercise rule for an option that is never
    // exercised.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_s_star_call(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));

    // --- AMERICAN PUT >= EUROPEAN PUT, ALWAYS -----------------------------
    // Early exercise is an extra right, never a liability.  Checked at four
    // points spanning moneyness and rate.
    let ap1: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96);
    let ep1: f64 = bopm_european_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96);
    fails = fails + must(ap1 >= ep1);
    let ap2: f64 = bopm_american_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let ep2: f64 = bopm_european_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    fails = fails + must(ap2 >= ep2);
    let ap3: f64 = bopm_american_put(110.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96);
    let ep3: f64 = bopm_european_put(110.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96);
    fails = fails + must(ap3 >= ep3);
    let ap4: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5, 96);
    let ep4: f64 = bopm_european_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5, 96);
    fails = fails + must(ap4 >= ep4);

    // ... and STRICTLY greater where exercise genuinely binds.  A `>=` alone
    // would pass if the American branch were dead code, which is exactly the
    // mutation this file must catch.  These two checks are the ones that fail
    // if the early-exercise max() is removed.
    fails = fails + must(ap1 > ep1);
    fails = fails + must(ap2 > ep2);

    // --- THE PREMIUM IS NON-NEGATIVE --------------------------------------
    // Same claim in the form the premium function actually returns, so a sign
    // error inside `early_exercise_premium_*` is caught rather than only the
    // ordering of the two prices.
    fails = fails + must(early_exercise_premium_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96) >= 0.0);
    fails = fails + must(early_exercise_premium_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96) >= 0.0);
    fails = fails + must(early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.08, 0.25, 1.0, 96) >= 0.0);
    // At q = 0 the CALL premium is not merely non-negative, it is EXACTLY
    // zero — the same Merton statement as above, routed through the subtraction
    // so a premium function that returned a small spurious value is caught.
    fails = fails + must(early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96) == 0.0);

    // --- THE PREMIUM INCREASES WITH THE DRIVER OF EARLY EXERCISE ----------
    // For a CALL the driver is the DIVIDEND YIELD: you exercise early to
    // capture a dividend you would otherwise forgo.  No dividend, no reason.
    // Measured at n=96, S=K=100, r=0.05, sigma=0.25, T=1:
    //   q=0.00 -> 0.0            q=0.02 -> 2.285e-06     q=0.05 -> 0.11077
    //   q=0.08 -> 0.43522        q=0.12 -> 0.90154
    // A strictly increasing chain, asserted as such.  This is a monotonicity
    // theorem, so it needs no reference value and survives a bad oracle; it is
    // also the check that would catch a sign error on q inside the carry term,
    // which a single-point value check at one q could easily miss.
    let cq0: f64 = early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let cq2: f64 = early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.02, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let cq5: f64 = early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let cq8: f64 = early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.08, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let cq12: f64 = early_exercise_premium_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.12, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    fails = fails + must(cq2 > cq0);
    fails = fails + must(cq5 > cq2);
    fails = fails + must(cq8 > cq5);
    fails = fails + must(cq12 > cq8);

    // For a PUT the driver is the INTEREST RATE: you exercise early to receive
    // the strike now and earn interest on it.  Zero rate, almost no reason.
    // Measured at n=96, S=K=100, q=0, sigma=0.25, T=1:
    //   r=0.00 -> 3.553e-15      r=0.02 -> 0.18223       r=0.05 -> 0.52988
    //   r=0.08 -> 0.88671        r=0.12 -> 1.32396
    //
    // NOTE THE r=0 VALUE: 3.553e-15, not 0.0.  The theory says the premium is
    // exactly zero at r=q=0, and the lattice says it is 4 ulp of the price.
    // That residue is the honest accumulated rounding of ~4700 dependent node
    // evaluations, and it is reported here rather than hidden: the r=0 check
    // below is `>= 0.0` and a `chk_abs(.., 0.0, 1e-14)`, NOT `== 0.0`.  An
    // equality there would be a false claim about what f64 lattice arithmetic
    // delivers.  Contrast the CALL case above, which IS asserted `== 0.0` —
    // that one is exact because the max() never binds at all, so the two
    // lattices are bit-identical rather than merely close.  The asymmetry
    // between those two assertions is real and is the point.
    let pr0: f64 = early_exercise_premium_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let pr2: f64 = early_exercise_premium_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.02, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let pr5: f64 = early_exercise_premium_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let pr8: f64 = early_exercise_premium_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    let pr12: f64 = early_exercise_premium_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.12, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 96);
    fails = fails + must(pr0 >= 0.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(pr0, 0.0, 0.00000000000001));
    fails = fails + must(pr2 > pr0);
    fails = fails + must(pr5 > pr2);
    fails = fails + must(pr8 > pr5);
    fails = fails + must(pr12 > pr8);

    // --- AN AMERICAN PUT IS NEVER WORTH LESS THAN INTRINSIC ---------------
    // It can be exercised right now, so K - S is a hard floor.  A deep ITM put
    // with little time value sits ON that floor, exactly.
    fails = fails + must(bopm_american_put(60.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96) >= 40.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bopm_american_put(60.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96), 40.0, 0.0000000001));
    // ... and never more than the strike itself (the underlying cannot go
    // below zero, so K is the most the payoff can ever be).
    fails = fails + must(bopm_american_put(60.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 96) <= 100.0);

    // --- CONVERGENCE, AND THE SECOND DIFFERENCE ---------------------------
    // Two distinct claims, and only the second one is worth much:
    //
    //   (a) the price at N and 2N agree within a band.  Measured, American
    //       put S=K=100 r=.05 sig=.2 T=1:  n=32 -> 6.063653547875674,
    //       n=64 -> 6.077526066883204, n=128 -> 6.084087099109399,
    //       n=256 -> 6.087258327709413.  The 128->256 gap is 0.00317, so a
    //       0.004 band is tight enough to mean something and loose enough to
    //       be honest about O(1/n) convergence.
    //
    //   (b) DOUBLING N MOVES THE ANSWER LESS THAN THE PREVIOUS DOUBLING DID.
    //       This is the check that says "converging" rather than "two numbers
    //       happen to be close".  Measured: |P64-P32| = 0.013873,
    //       |P128-P64| = 0.006561 (0.473x), |P256-P128| = 0.003171 (0.483x).
    //       Roughly halving each step, the expected O(1/n) rate for a CRR
    //       lattice on an American put.  A lattice with a systematic bias — a
    //       mis-set u, d or p — still produces two close numbers and still
    //       passes (a); it fails (b), because a biased sequence stops
    //       shrinking toward its own limit at the right rate.
    let l32: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 32);
    let l64: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64);
    let l128: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 128);
    let l256: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 256);
    fails = fails + must(absf(l256 - l128) <= 0.004);
    let g1: f64 = absf(l64 - l32);
    let g2: f64 = absf(l128 - l64);
    let g3: f64 = absf(l256 - l128);
    fails = fails + must(g2 < g1);
    fails = fails + must(g3 < g2);
    // The sequence is also monotone increasing here, which is a separate fact
    // from the gaps shrinking and catches a lattice that oscillates about the
    // limit rather than approaching it from one side.
    fails = fails + must(l64 > l32);
    fails = fails + must(l128 > l64);
    fails = fails + must(l256 > l128);

    // --- DETERMINISM: the same inputs twice give the SAME BITS -------------
    // A run-to-run check within one substrate.  It catches a lattice that
    // depended on uninitialised heap or on an allocation address.  The
    // cross-substrate half is the CI fixture, not this file.
    let d1: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 77);
    let d2: f64 = bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 77);
    fails = fails + must(__mind_f64_to_bits(d1) == __mind_f64_to_bits(d2));
    let b1: f64 = baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    let b2: f64 = baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0);
    fails = fails + must(__mind_f64_to_bits(b1) == __mind_f64_to_bits(b2));

    // =====================================================================
    // 2. VALUE CHECKS.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- EUROPEAN leg: exact closed form, 50-digit oracle -----------------
    // Band 1024 ulp.  This leg goes through dm_log, dm_sqrt, dm_exp and
    // dm_erfc; the band absorbs the honest difference between a re-derived
    // erfc and the oracle, and nothing more.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(eu_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 10.450583572185568, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(eu_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.573526022256968, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(eu_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5), 3.785353707650693, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(eu_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0), 8.456834637497863, 1024.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(eu_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0), 9.22086464770246, 1024.0));

    // --- LATTICE leg: same discretisation at 50 digits --------------------
    // READ THE PROVENANCE NOTE ABOVE.  These are NOT exact American prices and
    // are not claimed to be.  They are what THIS lattice, at THIS step count,
    // with THIS node-spot rule, evaluates to in 50-digit arithmetic.  Passing
    // proves the f64 implementation accumulates no meaningful error over the
    // ~33000 dependent node evaluations at n=256; it says nothing at all about
    // whether n=256 is close to the true American price.  Only the CONVERGENCE
    // block above speaks to that.
    //
    // Bands scale with n because the accumulated error does: 16384 ulp at
    // n=64 (~4e-12 absolute), 65536 ulp at n=256 (~9e-11 absolute).  Those are
    // the honest sizes; the ADVERSARIAL block below proves they still reject a
    // wrong value by a wide margin.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64), 6.077526066883306, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 128), 6.084087099109571, 32768.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 256), 6.087258327708621, 65536.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5, 64), 4.182241957779688, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5, 256), 4.189208703046372, 65536.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0, 64), 8.745942037766113, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0, 256), 8.762638013408512, 65536.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_call(90.0, 100.0, 0.03, 0.06, 0.3, 0.5, 64), 3.5258665949595254, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 64), 11.642001711181535, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 256), 11.640895641904518, 65536.0));
    // The lattice EUROPEAN leg too, because the cross-check that the premium is
    // a difference of two same-discretisation prices depends on it.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_european_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64), 10.419400102763714, 16384.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bopm_european_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64), 5.542342552835113, 16384.0));

    // --- BAW closed-form approximation vs the 50-digit oracle -------------
    // This checks the APPROXIMATION IS IMPLEMENTED CORRECTLY, which is a
    // completely different question from whether the approximation is accurate.
    // The oracle ran the identical algorithm — same q1/q2 roots, same seed,
    // same FIXED 64-step Newton — at 50 digits.  Agreement here means the MIND
    // code computes the BAW formula right.  How far BAW is from the true
    // American price is measured separately, in the CROSS-CHECK block.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 6.097615380989132, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5), 4.193351175892587, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0), 11.579761208703776, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0), 8.80726918878011, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_call(90.0, 100.0, 0.03, 0.06, 0.3, 0.5), 3.5584905940579463, 4096.0));

    // --- The critical boundary S* itself ----------------------------------
    // Checked as a VALUE, because an S* that is wrong but plausible would shift
    // every BAW price by a small amount that the price bands might absorb.
    // Oracle: same fixed 64-step Newton at 50 digits.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 81.6954336531575, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5), 87.24802019893882, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0), 81.86835725845292, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_s_star_call(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0), 139.16355000592196, 4096.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(baw_s_star_call(100.0, 0.03, 0.06, 0.3, 0.5), 136.7705749753014, 4096.0));

    // --- STRUCTURAL properties of the boundary, which need no oracle ------
    // A put is exercised early only when deep enough in the money, so the put
    // boundary lies strictly below the strike; a call boundary lies strictly
    // above it.  A sign error that swapped the two roots would still produce a
    // plausible number and would fail here.
    fails = fails + must(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) < 100.0);
    fails = fails + must(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) > 0.0);
    fails = fails + must(baw_s_star_call(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0) > 100.0);
    // A shorter-dated put has less time value to give up, so its exercise
    // boundary is CLOSER to the strike.  A monotonicity claim in T, oracle-free.
    fails = fails + must(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.25) > baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 2.0));
    // At or below the boundary BAW must return exactly intrinsic: that is the
    // definition of the boundary, and it tests the dispatch branch rather than
    // the formula.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(baw_put(50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 50.0, 0.0000000000001));

    // =====================================================================
    // 3. CROSS-CHECK: BAW against a converged lattice.
    //
    // Two structurally independent methods — an optimal-stopping lattice and a
    // closed-form quadratic approximation — priced on the same inputs.  A bug
    // in one would have to be matched by an equal and opposite bug in the other
    // to survive this, which is why it is worth more than either method's own
    // value check.
    //
    // THE BANDS ARE THE APPROXIMATION ERROR, MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN FOR
    // CONVENIENCE.  n=256 lattice vs BAW:
    //     ATM put  S=K=100 r=.05 sig=.20 T=1    lat 6.087258  baw 6.097615  +0.010357  0.170%
    //     ATM put  S=K=100 r=.08 sig=.20 T=.5   lat 4.189209  baw 4.193351  +0.004142  0.099%
    //     ITM put  S=90 K=100 r=.10 sig=.25 T=1 lat 11.640896 baw 11.579761 -0.061134  0.525%
    //     ATM call S=K=100 r=.05 q=.07 T=1      lat 8.762638  baw 8.807269  +0.044631  0.509%
    //
    // Each band below is the measured gap rounded up modestly — NOT rounded up
    // to a round number that would hide a regression.  The worst case, the
    // deep-ITM high-rate put at 0.53%, is exactly the regime where BAW's
    // dropped PDE term stops being small, and its band is the widest for that
    // stated reason rather than because it was failing.
    //
    // IF ONE OF THESE FAILS IN FUTURE, THAT IS A FINDING.  The correct response
    // is to investigate which of the two methods moved, never to widen the
    // band.  A widened band here silently converts a real disagreement between
    // two independent methods into a green check, which is the single most
    // damaging thing a reader of this file could do to it.
    //
    // Note the SIGN structure, which is itself informative: BAW is above the
    // lattice for the two ATM puts and the dividend call, and BELOW it for the
    // deep-ITM put.  BAW's error is not a uniform bias, so a check that only
    // asserted `baw >= lattice` would be wrong.  Absolute bands it is.
    // =====================================================================
    fails = fails + must(absf(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) - bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 256)) <= 0.011);
    fails = fails + must(absf(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5) - bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.08, 0.0, 0.2, 0.5, 256)) <= 0.005);
    fails = fails + must(absf(baw_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0) - bopm_american_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 256)) <= 0.065);
    fails = fails + must(absf(baw_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0) - bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0, 256)) <= 0.047);
    // The q=0 call is the one case where BAW is EXACT rather than approximate
    // (Merton again: the approximation branch is never taken), so the lattice
    // must approach it at the lattice's own discretisation error, ~0.03 at
    // n=256, not at BAW's approximation error.  A different band for a
    // different reason, stated so.
    fails = fails + must(absf(baw_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) - bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 256)) <= 0.035);

    // =====================================================================
    // 4. DOMAIN CHECKS: degenerate inputs return the canonical qNaN.
    // A zero-vol or expired option is a caller error here, and the function
    // says so instead of guessing a plausible number.
    // =====================================================================
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(0.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(0.0 - 10.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(100.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(100.0, 0.0 - 5.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0 - 0.2, 1.0, 64)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 0 - 3)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 2000)));
    // A lattice too coarse for the volatility puts p outside [0,1]; that is an
    // arbitrageable tree and must be refused, not priced.  Two probes: p~791 is
    // the easy case, p~1.0326 is the one that actually exercises the bound.  A
    // mutation loosening `p > 1.0` to `p > 2.0` survives the first and is
    // caught only by the second.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 5.0, 0.0, 0.01, 1.0, 2)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.15, 0.0, 0.1, 1.0, 2)));
    // The closed-form surface has the same duty.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(eu_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(eu_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_put(0.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_put(100.0, 0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.2, 0.0)));
    // r <= 0 has no early-exercise incentive for a put and the BAW boundary
    // equation degenerates (M = 2r/sigma^2 = 0).  Refused rather than iterated.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(baw_s_star_put(0.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0)));
    // dm_powpos refuses a non-positive base rather than returning a plausible
    // number through log(0) = -inf.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(dm_powpos(0.0, 2.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(dm_powpos(0.0 - 4.0, 0.5)));

    // =====================================================================
    // 5. ADVERSARIAL NEGATIVE CONTROLS.
    //
    // Every check below adds a SPURIOUS ACCEPTANCE straight into `fails`.  The
    // band must REJECT the wrong value; if it accepts, the run goes red.
    // Without this block none of the bands above prove anything — a band wide
    // enough to accept everything passes vacuously, and there is no way to tell
    // that apart from a correct implementation by reading a green result.
    //
    // The perturbations are deliberately SMALL relative to the quantity — a
    // wrong value that a careless band would swallow — rather than absurd ones
    // that any band rejects.  A negative control that is too easy proves as
    // little as no control at all.
    // =====================================================================

    // European leg: 1e-5 relative, ~4.5e10 ulp, far outside the 1024-ulp band.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(eu_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 10.450683572185568, 1024.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(eu_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 5.573626022256968, 1024.0);

    // Lattice leg: perturbed in the 7th decimal.  The n=256 band is 65536 ulp
    // ~ 9e-11 absolute, so a 1e-6 error is ~10000x the band — this control has
    // real margin and is not scraping the edge.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 256), 6.087259327708621, 65536.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bopm_american_put(90.0, 100.0, 0.1, 0.0, 0.25, 1.0, 64), 11.642002711181535, 16384.0);

    // BAW leg and the boundary.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 6.097625380989132, 4096.0);
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 81.6964336531575, 4096.0);

    // A WRONG S* by 1%: exactly the plausible-but-wrong boundary the value
    // check above exists to catch.  If this were accepted, a Newton iteration
    // that had silently stopped converging would pass the whole file.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(baw_s_star_put(100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0), 82.5123879896891, 4096.0);

    // The CROSS-CHECK bands must reject a disagreement larger than BAW's known
    // error.  These are the adversarial form of the cross-check: they prove the
    // bands are narrow enough to detect a method actually going wrong, rather
    // than merely wide enough to pass today.  A BAW that was off by 0.2 on the
    // ATM put (20x its true error) must NOT be accepted by the 0.011 band.
    fails = fails + must(absf((baw_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0) + 0.2) - bopm_american_put(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.0, 0.2, 1.0, 256)) > 0.011);
    fails = fails + must(absf((baw_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0) - 0.3) - bopm_american_call(100.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 0.25, 1.0, 256)) > 0.047);

    // A zero premium must FAIL the non-trivial-premium claim: the adversarial
    // form of "the American put is STRICTLY worth more".  An implementation
    // whose early-exercise max() never bound would report premium 0 here and
    // this is what refuses it.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(0.0, 0.5298831586751325, 0.00000000000001);

    // And the mirror of the Merton check: a premium that is NOT zero at q=0
    // for a call must be rejected.  This proves the `== 0.0` assertion above
    // discriminates rather than comparing something to itself.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(0.0001, 0.0, 0.00000000000001);

    return fails;
}
